   "text": "I read the molecule as a macrocycle and wrote it out in four parts.\n\n**Macrocycle backbone.** It runs in this order:\n- a secondary amine NH on the left;\n- the first ornithine \u03b1-carbon;\n- C(=O)\u2013NH;\n- the second ornithine \u03b1-carbon;\n- C(=O)\u2013N(CH3);\n- the tryptophan \u03b1-carbon;\n- C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013CH2;\n- a benzene ring;\n- S;\n- a pyridine ring;\n- CH2, which closes back to the amine NH.\n\n**Side chains.**\n- Each ornithine carries a CH2CH2CH2\u2013NH\u2013Boc side chain.\n- The tryptophan CH2 is attached to indol-3-yl, whose ring nitrogen carries a Boc group.\n\n**Aromatic rings.**\n- The pyridine is attached to the S at C2 and to the CH2\u2013N at C3.\n- On the benzene, the S-bearing carbon is next to the carbon bearing the CH2\u2013NH group, and that carbon is next to the carbon bearing Br.\n\n**Stereochemistry.** All three \u03b1-carbons have wedge bonds. Assigning CIP priorities (N > C=O > side-chain CH2 > H) with H pointing away gives S at each centre, consistent with L-amino acids. In the SMILES this corresponds to `@` for the first ornithine (written with its ring-closure N) and `@@` for the second ornithine and the tryptophan.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)(C)OC(=O)NCCC[C@H]1C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCNC(=O)OC(C)(C)C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](Cc2cn(C(=O)OC(C)(C)C)c3ccccc23)C(=O)NCc4c(Br)cccc4Sc5ncccc5CN1"
